.TH READLINK 1
.SH NAME
readlink \- print the target of a symbolic link, optionally canonicalized
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B readlink
.RB [ \-femnqsvz ]
.RB [ \-\- ]
.I file
.RI [ file ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
By default
.B readlink
prints the contents of each symbolic-link
.IR file
(the value returned by
.BR readlink (2))
to the standard output.  It is an error if
.I file
is not a symbolic link.
.PP
With one of the canonicalization options,
.B readlink
instead resolves
.I file
to an absolute pathname, following every symbolic link encountered along
the way.  Multiple
.I file
operands may be given (BSD and GNU); each result is printed on its own
line.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-canonicalize
Canonicalize
.IR file ,
following all symbolic links.  Every component except the last must
exist (FreeBSD
.BR realpath (3)
semantics).
.TP
.BR \-e ", " \-\-canonicalize\-existing
Like
.BR \-f ,
but every component, including the last, must exist.
.TP
.BR \-m ", " \-\-canonicalize\-missing
Like
.BR \-f ,
but no component need exist; non-existent components are resolved
lexically.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-no\-newline
Do not output the trailing separator.  With several operands no
separators are written at all.
.TP
.BR \-z ", " \-\-zero
Separate output entries with a NUL byte rather than a newline.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet ", " \-s ", " \-\-silent
Suppress error messages.  This is the default.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Report errors.
.TP
.BR \-\-help
Print a usage message and exit.
.TP
.BR \-\-version
Print version information and exit.
.PP
Option processing stops at
.B \-\-
or at the first operand; unlike GNU, options are not permuted past an
operand (BSD/POSIX behaviour).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B readlink
exits 0 if every operand was resolved successfully, and >0 otherwise.
.SH EXAMPLES
Read a symbolic link:
.PP
.B readlink /usr/bin/cc
.PP
Resolve a pathname to its canonical absolute form (the idiom used by
.BR starttde (1)
to locate its own installation):
.PP
.B readlink \-f \-\- "$0"
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR dirname (1),
.BR basename (1),
.BR readlink (2),
.BR realpath (3)
.SH STANDARDS
The default mode is POSIX/BSD/GNU compatible.  The
.BR \-f ", " \-e ", " \-m ", " \-z
options follow GNU coreutils and the
.BR \-f ", " \-n
options follow BSD; where BSD and GNU disagree, BSD behaviour is
preferred.
